1
00:00:18,003 --> 00:00:29,909
[SPEAKER_03]: The main reason why the Buddha would say... if he wanted to describe the worst type of suffering, he would say, like, pains of dying... death or those of dying.

2
00:00:31,370 --> 00:00:33,431
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because it's physically unpleasant.

3
00:00:34,231 --> 00:00:36,012
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not why those pains are there.

4
00:00:36,072 --> 00:00:36,973
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the mental anguish.

5
00:00:38,934 --> 00:00:45,057
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what's the... what's the main characteristic of the...

6
00:00:46,385 --> 00:00:49,847
[SPEAKER_03]: Discomfort of death or thoughts of dying.

7
00:00:50,528 --> 00:00:56,091
[SPEAKER_03]: What's that, the most unpleasant aspect of it?

8
00:00:56,111 --> 00:00:58,013
[SPEAKER_03]: How can you describe it?

9
00:00:58,313 --> 00:00:58,933
[SPEAKER_02]: Non-control.

10
00:00:59,354 --> 00:01:00,414
[SPEAKER_03]: How is that manifested?

11
00:01:02,035 --> 00:01:05,197
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, when you don't have control at all.

12
00:01:06,198 --> 00:01:08,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it threatens your existence.

13
00:01:08,199 --> 00:01:08,439
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

14
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:12,462
[SPEAKER_01]: It threatens yourself, your sense... as in...

15
00:01:13,203 --> 00:01:15,184
[SPEAKER_03]: So, which... what example?

16
00:01:15,688 --> 00:01:28,717
[SPEAKER_03]: What then can you experience in the world that would resemble that ultimate threat of death, while you're still alive, that would partake in exactly the same nature?

17
00:01:30,679 --> 00:01:31,559
[SPEAKER_02]: Losing your memory.

18
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:32,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry?

19
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:34,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Sickness.

20
00:01:34,601 --> 00:01:35,682
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, all of these.

21
00:01:35,822 --> 00:01:38,924
[SPEAKER_03]: All of these things, they all have this one thread in common.

22
00:01:39,385 --> 00:01:40,225
[SPEAKER_03]: Imprisonment.

23
00:01:40,265 --> 00:01:40,586
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

24
00:01:40,626 --> 00:01:40,806
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

25
00:01:40,886 --> 00:01:41,446
[SPEAKER_03]: Imprisonment.

26
00:01:42,347 --> 00:01:43,488
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the main

27
00:01:44,237 --> 00:01:47,278
[SPEAKER_03]: The main characteristic of imprisonment is confinement.

28
00:01:49,018 --> 00:01:52,599
[SPEAKER_03]: So, generally, confinement is the worst.

29
00:01:53,420 --> 00:02:04,823
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, you can have it a little bit more so, but the worst possible idea, the worst phobias, are the ideas of when you're confined, you can't move, you can't breathe, you can't do anything.

30
00:02:05,243 --> 00:02:07,064
[SPEAKER_03]: So, confined within your own body.

31
00:02:07,984 --> 00:02:11,165
[SPEAKER_03]: And you can get the glimpses of that throughout

32
00:02:12,293 --> 00:02:14,035
[SPEAKER_03]: Life, on account of this or that.

33
00:02:14,815 --> 00:02:29,590
[SPEAKER_03]: But usually, the view will kind of keep it pinned to the life and circumstances, failing to understand that possibility of confinement will ultimately be exemplified for you through the experience of death or those of dying.

34
00:02:30,751 --> 00:02:32,512
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly why the Buddha would describe it.

35
00:02:32,532 --> 00:02:41,836
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the reason why death and those of dying, pains like those of dying, is so unpleasant, it's because of the confinement that the mind naturally resists, craves against.

36
00:02:42,516 --> 00:02:44,257
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because it's unpleasant in and of itself.

37
00:02:45,338 --> 00:02:50,560
[SPEAKER_03]: It might be uncomfortable, but the mental anguish does not come automatically with it.

38
00:02:51,281 --> 00:02:56,123
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the mental anguish of the mind that it's now facing its confinement.

39
00:02:58,888 --> 00:03:01,591
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the worst fear and that's the worst pain.

40
00:03:02,271 --> 00:03:12,542
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if the mind learns how not to move amidst confinement and everything, these examples of confinements, then eventually even death will not be able to move it, because it's exactly the same thing.

41
00:03:13,082 --> 00:03:14,584
[SPEAKER_03]: You're confined within your own senses.

42
00:03:15,585 --> 00:03:18,948
[SPEAKER_03]: They belong to matter, eyes, sights, ears, nose, everything.

43
00:03:20,256 --> 00:03:26,044
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's a confinement that you don't see, because, well, it's confining in this prison, but look at all the joys and pleasures I get.

44
00:03:26,324 --> 00:03:29,649
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, well, yeah, but because of those joys you are confined, not the other way around.

45
00:03:30,931 --> 00:03:36,679
[SPEAKER_03]: And once you start seeing that, then you realize, okay, these are just baits that keep me confined in this environment.

46
00:03:41,859 --> 00:03:44,900
[SPEAKER_02]: To every suffering that you have is that you're not getting what you want.

47
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:46,421
[SPEAKER_02]: You're confined.

48
00:03:46,441 --> 00:03:49,862
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a limit to what you can get, what yourself wants to appropriate.

49
00:03:50,483 --> 00:03:51,043
[SPEAKER_02]: Confinement.

50
00:03:51,303 --> 00:03:52,343
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not getting what I...

51
00:03:53,064 --> 00:03:54,144
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm getting what I don't want.

52
00:03:54,284 --> 00:03:56,165
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm crushed in.

53
00:03:56,305 --> 00:03:56,485
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

54
00:03:56,965 --> 00:04:06,709
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why I kept saying in the past as well, like, if a person wants to contemplate death, you don't contemplate it in these abstract terms or impersonal looking at the dead bodies and so on.

55
00:04:06,729 --> 00:04:08,450
[SPEAKER_03]: No, just find things that confine you.

56
00:04:10,361 --> 00:04:12,362
[SPEAKER_03]: Sense restraint will be confining.

57
00:04:12,523 --> 00:04:14,764
[SPEAKER_03]: Saying no to your desires will be very confining.

58
00:04:15,625 --> 00:04:18,507
[SPEAKER_03]: Doing things you don't want to do in terms of duties will be very confining.

59
00:04:19,929 --> 00:04:23,632
[SPEAKER_03]: Do that, and see whether your mind moves or not, and how much, and that's your training.

60
00:04:24,112 --> 00:04:27,275
[SPEAKER_03]: Not allow your mind to move amidst things that want to move you.

61
00:04:27,715 --> 00:04:32,759
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the... that eventually can result in that opening within the confinement.

62
00:04:32,919 --> 00:04:36,923
[SPEAKER_03]: Not left, right, below, or above it.

63
00:04:37,921 --> 00:04:45,407
[SPEAKER_03]: So, while fully confined, the mind is released from it, turned away from it, dispatched through dispassion.

64
00:04:45,507 --> 00:04:45,947
[SPEAKER_03]: No other way.

65
00:04:46,908 --> 00:04:47,568
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no other way.

66
00:04:47,728 --> 00:04:51,331
[SPEAKER_03]: There's not a mystical portal that you can access through.

67
00:04:51,411 --> 00:04:56,075
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just through sheer dispassion amidst the confinement and discomfort of confinement.

68
00:04:56,976 --> 00:05:04,942
[SPEAKER_03]: If you have wisdom, patience, that confinement can be gradually built up, so you will not necessarily suffer so sharply

69
00:05:06,122 --> 00:05:07,443
[SPEAKER_03]: Immediately, for a long time.

70
00:05:08,603 --> 00:05:10,644
[SPEAKER_03]: But, either way, sometimes you just have to.

71
00:05:11,585 --> 00:05:12,965
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's not up to you to decide.

72
00:05:14,046 --> 00:05:26,912
[SPEAKER_03]: As the Buddha said, for somebody, the path towards that opening, that freedom, is going to be quick and unpleasant, quick and pleasant, slow and pleasant, and slow and unpleasant.

73
00:05:28,433 --> 00:05:33,095
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you'll only... Yeah, you will know that only in hindsight, which way it was.

74
00:05:33,756 --> 00:05:34,176
[SPEAKER_03]: And again,

75
00:05:34,877 --> 00:05:46,284
[SPEAKER_03]: I think, if we were to bet on which way most people would be, two and a half millennia after the Buddha, probably it's going to be slow and unpleasant for most of... for most of... mostly everyone, yeah.

76
00:05:48,746 --> 00:05:56,350
[SPEAKER_03]: But again, even that displeasure does not mean it's going to be this excruciating torment by default, automatically.

77
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:56,530
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

78
00:05:56,690 --> 00:06:00,353
[SPEAKER_03]: That is determined by how much you actively crave against it.

79
00:06:01,438 --> 00:06:07,619
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not like a pool of pain that you have to sort of drain and just endure through.

80
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:26,803
[SPEAKER_03]: No, it's... you could technically abandon it right there and then, if you're able to face it for what it is, keep your mind dispassionate towards it, and do that endurance that we talked about, in regard to that phenomenon of confinement.

81
00:06:28,444 --> 00:06:29,884
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, everything else will just fall away.

82
00:06:44,937 --> 00:06:45,778
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what dukkha is.

83
00:06:45,798 --> 00:06:46,698
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't find it.

84
00:06:46,818 --> 00:06:46,998
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

85
00:06:47,718 --> 00:06:52,420
[SPEAKER_03]: Not getting what you want, being separated from what you want, being united with what you don't want.

86
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:55,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you want it or not.

87
00:06:56,701 --> 00:06:57,021
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

88
00:06:57,921 --> 00:06:58,141
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

89
00:06:58,802 --> 00:07:01,362
[SPEAKER_02]: Having... there being a body, there being experience.

90
00:07:01,402 --> 00:07:01,963
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, exactly.

91
00:07:02,003 --> 00:07:03,923
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, once you start looking at it closely...

92
00:07:03,983 --> 00:07:04,263
[SPEAKER_03]: Both.

93
00:07:04,804 --> 00:07:08,905
[SPEAKER_03]: Or authentically, you recognize your own eyes, nose, senses.

94
00:07:09,736 --> 00:07:11,177
[SPEAKER_03]: You had no say in their arising.

95
00:07:11,197 --> 00:07:13,859
[SPEAKER_03]: You certainly had no say in what they want and their desires.

96
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:20,524
[SPEAKER_03]: You're just fully and perpetually subjected to it, pressured by it, confined by it.

97
00:07:21,525 --> 00:07:24,747
[SPEAKER_03]: Confined by the duty of maintaining and feeding your desires.

98
00:07:25,288 --> 00:07:27,309
[SPEAKER_03]: Otherwise, the mind will just become too unbearable.

99
00:07:30,392 --> 00:07:34,675
[SPEAKER_03]: And, yeah, that's... that's like a hard labor prison camp.

100
00:07:36,756 --> 00:07:38,558
[SPEAKER_03]: You also have to work all the time.

101
00:07:42,851 --> 00:07:55,103
[SPEAKER_02]: So, recognizing your confinement, the extent of confinement that you are in, you're seeing, understanding dukkha, then, feeling it.

102
00:07:55,123 --> 00:07:57,305
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yeah, I think pretty much, yeah.

103
00:07:58,254 --> 00:08:01,856
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, but you actually have to... Recognizing then what brings that dukkha.

104
00:08:02,236 --> 00:08:03,797
[SPEAKER_03]: You realize, well, it's not the confinement.

105
00:08:04,698 --> 00:08:08,480
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, then you recognize the dukkha, but it's not the dukkha anymore.

106
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:12,903
[SPEAKER_03]: You recognize the dukkha, then you recognize the source of dukkha, then you recognize the escape from dukkha.

107
00:08:14,403 --> 00:08:21,948
[SPEAKER_02]: And the source is your fighting inside that, or fighting against that.

108
00:08:21,968 --> 00:08:22,808
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, resisting it.

109
00:08:23,429 --> 00:08:24,529
[SPEAKER_03]: Resisting confinement.

110
00:08:26,030 --> 00:08:27,531
[SPEAKER_03]: By default, at face value.

111
00:08:30,725 --> 00:08:48,815
[SPEAKER_03]: So, for as long as you have the view or views that, oh, you know, this unpleasant experience that I'm pressured by, confined by, is caused because of such and such thing happened, that's already view that looks past the confinement, blames it on something else.

112
00:08:49,855 --> 00:08:53,818
[SPEAKER_03]: So, first you just have to stop acting out of those views.

113
00:08:54,818 --> 00:08:57,760
[SPEAKER_03]: You might think, oh, I suffer because he said something that upset me,

114
00:08:58,482 --> 00:09:02,765
[SPEAKER_03]: But because I restrain, I don't fuel their view, and then their view actually falls away.

115
00:09:02,845 --> 00:09:05,167
[SPEAKER_03]: And I realize, well, I suffer because suffering arisen.

116
00:09:05,627 --> 00:09:06,007
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

117
00:09:07,609 --> 00:09:08,589
[SPEAKER_03]: Yesterday, he said something.

118
00:09:08,689 --> 00:09:09,930
[SPEAKER_03]: Another day, somebody else didn't do something.

119
00:09:09,950 --> 00:09:14,814
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's always something that's going to be seemingly the reason at the face value.

120
00:09:14,914 --> 00:09:17,356
[SPEAKER_03]: But fundamentally, I suffer because suffering is present.

121
00:09:18,677 --> 00:09:21,359
[SPEAKER_03]: I suffer because I resist confinement.

122
00:09:21,639 --> 00:09:21,979
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

123
00:09:23,473 --> 00:09:26,235
[SPEAKER_03]: Presence of resistance means suffering.

124
00:09:26,655 --> 00:09:29,357
[SPEAKER_03]: Absence of resistance means not suffering amidst confinement.

125
00:09:29,397 --> 00:09:30,898
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly what Sutta describes.

126
00:09:31,378 --> 00:09:34,961
[SPEAKER_03]: Those who understand this, they don't suffer amidst the suffering.

127
00:09:42,906 --> 00:09:49,471
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the extent of confinement reveals the extent of dukkha, because it shows the extent of your resistance towards it.

128
00:09:49,751 --> 00:09:52,193
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the first... well, the Four Noble Truths.

129
00:09:54,777 --> 00:10:00,859
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, how do you tame your mind to remove any trace of resistance within confinement?

130
00:10:01,039 --> 00:10:09,322
[SPEAKER_03]: Whether it's a confinement of desire, pressure to act out, any other type of confinement, how do you train your mind?

131
00:10:09,382 --> 00:10:10,702
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's the Noble Eighth Path.

132
00:10:11,162 --> 00:10:12,263
[SPEAKER_03]: You realize, okay, so it's not...

133
00:10:12,743 --> 00:10:18,525
[SPEAKER_03]: I can't decide it by will to tell this mind to stop resisting it, kicking and screaming like a wild animal.

134
00:10:19,212 --> 00:10:30,878
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if I adhere to certain behaviors, avoid certain other behaviors, cultivate skillful things, avoid unskillful things, eat suitable food, not unsuitable food, tame my senses, that animal will have to calm down.

135
00:10:31,719 --> 00:10:35,281
[SPEAKER_03]: And when that animal calms down, my view will be fulfilled.

136
00:10:36,061 --> 00:10:38,603
[SPEAKER_03]: View of freedom amidst confinement that I understood.

137
00:10:40,664 --> 00:10:46,667
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's like sotāpanna's understanding that can then lead to arahantship if he fully tames that mind within what he already understood.

